## Statement

Assume AC, $n>p\ge1$, and the iid multivariate-normal sample model with covariance $\Sigma\succ0$. For $\mu_0\in\mathbb R^p$, define $T^2=n(\bar X-\mu_0)^TS^{-1}(\bar X-\mu_0)$ when $S\succ0$, and set $T^2=0$ otherwise. Under $H_0:\mu=\mu_0$,
$$\frac{n-p}{p(n-1)}T^2\sim F_{p,n-p}.$$
The zero extension does not change the law because $S\succ0$ almost surely when $n>p$.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** AC, $n>p\ge1$, an iid $N_p(\mu,\Sigma)$ sample with $\Sigma\succ0$, and the null $\mu=\mu_0$.

[A1] AC is used in the Gaussian and Wishart constructions. [[def-axiom-of-choice]]

[F1] The sample mean is independent of the centered scatter and $(n-1)S\sim W_p(\Sigma,n-1)$; for $n>p$, $S\succ0$ almost surely. [[thm-multivariate-normal-sample-mean-scatter-independence-and-wishart]]

[F2] The Wishart law is the law of a sum of independent Gaussian outer products, with scale $\Sigma$ and integer degrees of freedom. [[def-wishart-law]]

[F3] A Gaussian vector with covariance $\Sigma$ is obtained by multiplying a standard normal by $\Sigma^{1/2}$. [[def-multivariate-normal-law]]

[F4] For independent $W\sim W_p(I_p,m)$ and $Z\sim N_p(0,I_p)$ with $m\ge p$, $W\succ0$ almost surely and $\frac{m-p+1}{p}Z^TW^{-1}Z\sim F_{p,m-p+1}$. [[lem-standard-wishart-independent-normal-quadratic-form-is-f]]

[F5] The Fisher law has numerator and denominator degrees of freedom as given by its independent chi-square ratio definition. [[def-student-t-and-f-laws]] [[def-chi-square-law]]

[F6] Positive-degree chi-square variables are positive almost surely, so the Fisher denominator is positive. [[lem-positive-df-chi-square-has-density-and-regular-cdf]]

[F7] Every positive-semidefinite symmetric matrix has a positive square root; if $\Sigma\succ0$ and $\Sigma^{1/2}v=0$, then $v^T\Sigma v=\|\Sigma^{1/2}v\|^2=0$, so the root is injective and hence invertible. [[thm-non-negative-square-root-exists-and-is-unique]]

[F8] Measurable functions of independent random elements remain independent. [[lem-measurable-functions-preserve-independence]]

[F9] The sample map $S$ is continuous by the model definition, and Sylvester's criterion expresses $\mathcal S_{++}^p$ as finitely many strict polynomial inequalities; hence $\{S\succ0\}$ is Borel. Inversion is continuous on invertible matrices, and continuous Euclidean maps are Borel under countable choice, which follows here from AC. [[def-multivariate-normal-sample-model-and-statistics]] [[thm-sylvesters-criterion-for-positive-definiteness]] [[cor-continuous-functions-are-borel-measurable]] [[lem-ac-supplies-sequential-choices-for-probability-constructions]]

## Proof

1.1 The statistic $T^2$ in the Statement is a random variable: by [F9], $\{S\succ0\}$ is Borel; on this set inversion is continuous, so the displayed quadratic form is Borel, and setting it to zero on the Borel complement preserves measurability. Let $Z=\sqrt n\,\Sigma^{-1/2}(\bar X-\mu_0)$. The positive root is invertible by [F7]; under the null, [F1] and [F3] therefore give $Z\sim N_p(0,I_p)$. Moreover, $Z$ and $W:=\Sigma^{-1/2}(n-1)S\Sigma^{-1/2}$ are independent: $\bar X$ and $S$ are independent by [F1], and the separate whitening maps are continuous, hence Borel, so [F8] preserves independence. The congruence in [F1] and [F2] gives $W\sim W_p(I_p,n-1)$; it is positive definite almost surely by [F1]. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F7, F8, F9, given, algebra]

2.1 On the event $S\succ0$ (which has probability one by [F1]), substitute $S=\Sigma^{1/2}W\Sigma^{1/2}/(n-1)$ and $\bar X-\mu_0=\Sigma^{1/2}Z/\sqrt n$ into the definition. Cancelling the inverse congruence gives $$T^2=(n-1)Z^TW^{-1}Z.$$ [F1, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 Apply [F4] with $m=n-1\ge p$. It gives $\frac{n-p}{p}Z^TW^{-1}Z\sim F_{p,n-p}$. Multiplying the identity in step 2.1 by $(n-p)/(p(n-1))$ proves the asserted law. Since $S\succ0$ almost surely, the off-domain zero convention affects only a null event. [F1, F4, F5, F6, step 2.1, algebra] ∎

